diff --git a/apps/docs/content/docs/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/azure-key-vault.mdx b/apps/docs/content/docs/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/azure-key-vault.mdx
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4aec61d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/apps/docs/content/docs/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/azure-key-vault.mdx
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+---
+title: Azure Key Vault
+description: Sign with a non-exportable HSM-backed key held in Azure Key Vault.
+---
+
+import { Callout } from 'fumadocs-ui/components/callout';
+import { Step, Steps } from 'fumadocs-ui/components/steps';
+
+Azure Key Vault Premium (and Managed HSM) keeps the signing key in a hardware security module. The private key never leaves the vault: Documenso hashes the document locally and sends only the digest to Key Vault's `sign` operation.
+
+This is the usual way to hold a publicly trusted document-signing certificate (for example an Adobe AATL certificate), because certificate authorities issue those only for keys generated and kept in an HSM.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+{/* prettier-ignore */}
+<Steps>
+<Step>
+### Create the key and certificate request in Key Vault
+
+In a **Premium** vault, create a certificate with an external issuer and a non-exportable `RSA-HSM` key. Key Vault generates the key and gives you a CSR to send to your certificate authority.
+
+</Step>
+<Step>
+### Merge the issued certificate
+
+When the authority returns the certificate, merge it into the pending request (`az keyvault certificate pending merge`). Key Vault refuses a certificate that was not issued for the request's key.
+
+</Step>
+<Step>
+### Create an identity that can sign
+
+Create a service principal and give it the **Key Vault Crypto User** role, scoped to the one key rather than the whole vault. It needs nothing else: the certificate chain is supplied to Documenso directly, so it does not need to read the vault's secrets.
+
+</Step>
+</Steps>
+
+## Environment Variables
+
+| Variable                                             | Description                                                                  |
+| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_TRANSPORT`                     | Set to `azure-kv`                                                            |
+| `NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_AZURE_KV_KEY_ID`               | Full key identifier **including the version**                                |
+| `NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_AZURE_KV_CERT_CHAIN_FILE_PATH` | Path to a PEM file: the signing certificate first, then its intermediates    |
+| `NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_AZURE_KV_CERT_CHAIN_CONTENTS`  | Base64-encoded contents of the same PEM file (alternative to the path)       |
+| `AZURE_TENANT_ID`                                    | Tenant of the service principal                                              |
+| `AZURE_CLIENT_ID`                                    | Application (client) ID of the service principal                             |
+| `AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH`                      | Path to the service principal's certificate and private key (PEM)            |
+| `AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET`                                | Client secret, if you use one instead of a certificate                       |
+
+The `AZURE_*` variables are read by the Azure SDK's `EnvironmentCredential`.
+
+## Configuration Example
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_TRANSPORT=azure-kv
+NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_AZURE_KV_KEY_ID=https://my-vault.vault.azure.net/keys/documenso-signing/0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
+NEXT_PRIVATE_SIGNING_AZURE_KV_CERT_CHAIN_FILE_PATH=/opt/documenso/signing-chain.pem
+AZURE_TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
+AZURE_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
+AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH=/opt/documenso/azure-signer.pem
+```
+
+Get the versioned key identifier with:
+
+```bash
+az keyvault certificate show --vault-name my-vault -n documenso-signing --query kid -o tsv
+```
+
+<Callout type="warn">
+  The key identifier must include the version. Renewing a Key Vault certificate creates a new key version; an unversioned identifier would start signing with the new key while Documenso still embeds the old certificate, and every signature would fail validation.
+</Callout>
+
+## Startup check
+
+When the signer is first created, Documenso compares the public key in the signing certificate with the public key of the Key Vault key and refuses to sign if they differ. A certificate issued for a different key cannot produce valid signatures, so this fails loudly at the first seal instead of producing documents that every validator rejects.
+
+The certificate status endpoint (`/api/certificate-status`, also used by `/api/health`) checks only that the configured certificate is within its validity period. It runs on every health probe, so it does not call Key Vault.
+
+## Timestamps
+
+Configure a [timestamp server](/docs/self-hosting/configuration/signing-certificate/timestamp-server). Without one, signatures stop validating when the signing certificate expires.
